const meterCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Scansion: the beat map', note: 'Mark the stresses: da-DUM = iamb (to BE), DUM-da = trochee (HEADstrong), da-da-DUM = anapest, DUM-da-da = dactyl (SIL-ly po-ems). Meter is the underlying pulse pattern a poem sets up.' },
    { math: '2 · Substitution is expression', note: 'Poems establish a meter, then break it where it hurts. An iambic line that stumbles into a trochee enacts the stumble. "Never give all the heart" — Yeats\'s line starts stressed because the command is urgent.' },
    { math: '3 · The classic forms', note: 'Iambic pentameter (Shakespeare\'s engine: five iambs, da-DUM ×5), blank verse (unrhymed pentameter — Milton, Wordsworth), the sonnet (14 lines, a volta — the turn), terza rima (Dante\'s interlocking aba bcb c), the couplet (couplets snap arguments shut).' },
    { math: '4 · Free verse isn\'t formless', note: 'Whitman and the moderns dropped fixed meter for patterns of breath, image and line-break. Free verse still has form — it is chosen line by line rather than inherited (Eliot: "no verse is free for the man who wants to do a good job").' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/poetry-form-and-meter">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Poetry is <strong>language measured</strong>: verse organises speech into beats, line-breaks
    and sound patterns that make meaning physical. Scansion — marking the stresses — is the
    skill that lets you see where a poem follows its pattern and, more importantly, where and
    why it breaks it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the beat map, the substitutions, the forms, and free verse's hidden discipline" widget="equationStep" config={meterCfg} />

  <h2>Sound effects worth hearing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Alliteration, assonance, consonance:</strong> repeated consonants, vowels and endings bind lines by sound — "the furrow followed free" (Coleridge) is a wave in the mouth.</li>
    <li><strong>Onomatopoeia and rhythm-as-content:</strong> Tennyson\'s "murmuring of innumerable bees" mimics the sound; Pope\'s "When Ajax strives some rock\'s vast weight to throw, / The line too labours" makes the line strain like Ajax.</li>
    <li><strong>The volta:</strong> the sonnet's turn — Petrarchan (problem→solution at line 9) or Shakespearean (couplet twist at line 13). The form's built-in argument move.</li>
    <li><strong>Read aloud:</strong> meter is heard, not seen — every scansion dispute resolves in the ear. Poetry was oral technology for millennia before print.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fussell, Poetic Meter and Poetic Form; Hollander, Rhyme's Reason; the primary poems themselves.</p>
</TopicPage>
